%I A401524 #10 Nov 05 2026 15:27:03
%S A401524 3,7,11,16,22,25,28,32,38,43,48,51,54,57,60,64,70,75,80,85,90,95,100,
%T A401524 103,106,109,112,115,118,121,124,128,134,139,144,149,154,159,164,169,
%U A401524 174,179,184,189,194,199,204,207,210,213,216,219,222,225,228,231,234
%N A401524 Subword complexity (number of distinct blocks of length n) of A400826.
%C A401524 A400826 gives the Grundy values of the impartial game in which from even n the only move is to n/2, from odd n > 1 the moves are to 3n+1 or to (3n+1)/2, and 1 has no moves. Its closed formula defines it for all n >= 1, so these terms do not depend on the Collatz conjecture.
%C A401524 Apart from n = 1 and the powers of 2, A400826 coincides with a sliding-block recoding of the period-doubling sequence A096268, whose subword complexity is A275202. The factors of length n that occur with positive frequency are exactly the factors of that recoded sequence; there are A275202(n+1) of them. The remaining a(n) - A275202(n+1) factors have frequency zero and occur only in windows containing 1 or a power of 2.
%H A401524 Miquel Cerda, <a href="/A401524/b401524.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401524 Miguel Cerdá Bennassar, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23722394">Complejidad de factores de la sucesión de valores de Grundy del juego de Collatz con atajo</a>, Zenodo, 2026 (in Spanish).
%H A401524 Miroslava Poláková, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.08387">Complexity and invariant measure of the period-doubling subshift</a>, arXiv:1902.08387 [math.DS], 2019.
%F A401524 a(1) = 3, a(2) = 7, a(3) = 11. For n >= 4, write n = 2^m + q with 0 <= q < 2^m. Then
%F A401524 a(n) = 2^(m+2) if q = 0,
%F A401524 a(n) = 2^(m+2) + 5*q + 1 if 1 <= q < 2^(m-1),
%F A401524 a(n) = 5*2^m + 3*q - 1 if 2^(m-1) <= q < 2^m.
%F A401524 In particular a(2^m) = 4*2^m for m >= 2.
%e A401524 a(2) = 7: the blocks of length 2 are 01, 02, 11, 12 and 20, which also occur in the recoded period-doubling sequence, and 10 and 21, which occur only at positions (2^e - 1, 2^e) with e >= 2.
%o A401524 (Python)
%o A401524 def a(n):
%o A401524     if n <= 3: return [3, 7, 11][n-1]
%o A401524     m = n.bit_length() - 1; q = n - (1 << m)
%o A401524     if q == 0: return 4*n
%o A401524     if q < (1 << (m-1)): return 4*(1 << m) + 5*q + 1
%o A401524     return 5*(1 << m) + 3*q - 1
%Y A401524 Cf. A096268, A275202, A400826.
%K A401524 nonn,easy,new
%O A401524 1,1
%A A401524 _Miquel Cerda_, Oct 28 2026
